---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { IDENTITY } from "../lib/identity";

const NB = "not built yet";
---

<Base
  title={`NTP · ${IDENTITY.shortName}`}
  description="A stratum 1 NTP server from a surplus GPS-disciplined oscillator, an Intel i210 and chrony."
  heading="NTP"
  tagline="Stratum 1 time from a GPS-disciplined oscillator."
>
  <div class="wrap" style="margin-bottom:44px">
    <dl class="livestrip">
      <div><dt>server</dt><dd><span class="nb">not public yet</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>live data</dt><dd><span class="nb">{NB}</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>host</dt><dd><span class="nb">{NB}</span></dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="label"><span class="eyebrow">Hardware</span></div>
      <dl class="deflist">
        <dt>Reference</dt><dd>Samsung UCCM-LP: OCXO and a u-blox LEA-6T GPS receiver</dd>
        <dt>Timestamping</dt><dd>Intel i210, the PPS edge goes into SDP0</dd>
        <dt>Display</dt><dd>SSD1322 OLED, 256 × 64</dd>
        <dt>Host</dt><dd>Raspberry Pi CM4 on the official IO board: <span class="nb">{NB}</span></dd>
        <dt>Front panel</dt><dd>3D-printed in ASA for U9 of the <a href="/vieta">Vieta rack</a>: <span class="nb">{NB}</span></dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="label"><span class="eyebrow">How</span></div>
      <div class="stack-lg">
        <div>
          <h2 class="grp">Pulse</h2>
          <p>The UCCM's 1PPS output reaches the i210's SDP0 through a 1 kΩ resistor. The NIC's PHC timestamps every rising edge, and chrony reads those timestamps as an <code>extpps</code> reference clock.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="grp">Seconds</h2>
          <p>A pulse says when a second starts, not which second it is. The UCCM reports its time in SCPI over a serial port, not NMEA, so a small daemon listens on that port, reads the time and passes chrony the second over a socket. The UCCM counts GPS time, which runs 18 s ahead of UTC, and the daemon subtracts the difference.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="grp">Serving</h2>
          <p>chrony also timestamps the NTP packets themselves in the i210's hardware, not in the kernel.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="grp">Holdover</h2>
          <p>Without a GPS lock the UCCM switches to holdover and the oven keeps the frequency. When GPS returns, it reports a lock again about 14 minutes after its loop has settled.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
